# Spanish-accented English: loanword adaptation, and the first worked
# example of EPENTHESIS in this notation.
#
# The shipped american-english set takes one language's broad reading to
# its own narrow reading, and every rule in it is a substitution. This
# set does something else: it is a SOURCE language's phonology reshaping
# a form borrowed from another. That is where insertion earns its keep --
# a borrowed shape that the borrowing language cannot syllabify is
# repaired by putting a segment in, not by changing one.
#
# The input is a broad American English transcription. The output is what
# a Spanish phonology does with it. Nothing here is a claim about any
# individual speaker: this is the systematic part, the part that follows
# from Spanish having five vowels, no /v z ʃ θ ð h/, TWO rhotics, and no
# s+consonant onset.
#
# This file used to say "one rhotic tap", and that is simply false about
# Spanish: the tap and the trill are separate phonemes and the minimal
# pair is the one every grammar opens with, 'pero' [ˈpeɾo] beside 'perro'
# [ˈpero]. The single English /ɹ/ therefore does not have a single Spanish
# answer, and the rhotic block below is five rules because of it.
#
# Ordered, like every set here. Each rule sees the previous rule's
# output. Three orderings below are load-bearing and each is marked; the
# rest are independent, which is measured rather than assumed -- see
# tests/test_rule_sets.py, where TestHowMuchOrderingMattersAtAll sweeps
# every transposition of this set and asserts that the ones which move an
# answer are exactly the ones putting one of the three the wrong way
# round. So the count in this paragraph is a claim the suite checks.

# --- prothesis ----------------------------------------------------------
# Spanish permits no word-initial s+consonant onset, so a borrowed one
# gets a vowel in front of it: escuela, español, estrés, esnob, eslogan.
# English 'school' comes out [eskul], 'stop' [estap], 'snow' [esnoʊ].
#
# The context pins two REAL segments to the right of the gap -- the
# literal /s/ and then a consonant. That matters for more than accuracy.
# An insertion anchored only on '#' used to fire outside explicit word
# marks, because the scan found the gap on the far side of the mark too.
# Here the far-side gap has to match a literal /s/ next, and outside the
# word there is nothing to match, so the spurious site failed on its own
# terms. '#eskul#' is what this set gives for '#skul#', with one 'e' and
# in the right place. That is luck turned into a property, and it is swept
# in the tests.
#
# THAT DEFECT HAS SINCE BEEN CLOSED and the note is kept for the property,
# not the bug. This file said "'∅ -> ə / # _' on '#kæt#' gives
# 'ə#əkæt#ə'"; MEASURED, it now gives '#əkæt#'. A boundary run is one
# boundary and an insertion takes its INNER gap, since there is nothing
# outside the form to insert into (docs/rules.md, "A boundary run is one
# boundary"). So the context here is no longer load-bearing for that
# reason -- it is still load-bearing for the two below.
#
# Written as TWO rules, which needs saying. The class wanted is "/s/ plus
# a consonant that is not a glide": Spanish repairs /sp st sk sm sn sf sl/
# but not /sw sj/, where the glide is syllabified as the vowel /u i/ and
# no illegal cluster ever arises -- 'suéter', 'suiche', not *'esuéter'.
# '[-vowel -approximant]' excludes the glides, but it excludes /l/ with
# them, since /l/ is a lateral approximant. The class is a DISJUNCTION of
# two feature bundles, and a bracketed query is a conjunction, so it
# cannot be said once. This is the same limit that makes the shipped
# american-english set's tapping three rules instead of one, and it is
# worth being exact about which limit it is: an agreement variable says
# "the same value here and there", which is what the English nasal place
# assimilation needs and gets in one line. A disjunction is a different
# thing and there is no variable for it. A limit of the formalism here,
# not of the phonology. Do not "simplify" these to
# '[-vowel]' -- that spelling also fires on 'sweet' and 'suit'.
∅ -> e / # _ s [-vowel -approximant] ; prothesis (s + obstruent or nasal)
∅ -> e / # _ s [channel=lateral] ; prothesis (s + l)

# --- the segments Spanish does not have ---------------------------------
# Literals, deliberately. Each line is one phoneme's fate and there is no
# natural class that groups them -- what they share is absence from an
# inventory, which is not a feature. Writing '[manner=fricative
# voiced=+] -> [voiced=-]' would fold /v z/ together and then also catch
# /ʒ/ and /ð/ and send them somewhere wrong, so the class would be doing
# less work than it looks like it is doing, not more.
v -> b ; v is b
# ORDERING (1 of 3), and the quietest one in the file: the /s/ this line
# produces is a site the rhotic block below reads. 'Israel' is quoted
# there as a trill environment, and it is /ɪzɹeɪl/ on the way in -- so
# 'ɹ is the trill after s' sees an /s/ only because this rule has already
# put one there. Run it after the rhotic block and 'ɪzɹeɪl' comes out
# with a tap, which is the wrong rhotic in the word the block cites.
z -> s ; z is s
ʃ -> t͡ʃ ; ʃ is t͡ʃ
θ -> t ; θ is t
ð -> d ; ð is d
h -> x ; h is x

# Word-final /ŋ/ only. Spanish has [ŋ] as an allophone of /n/ before a
# velar, so 'sink' keeps its velar nasal by assimilation and needs no
# rule; what it has no room for is a velar nasal in the coda with nothing
# behind it, and 'sing' comes out [sin].
ŋ -> n / _ # ; final ŋ is n

# --- the rhotic ---------------------------------------------------------
# ORDERING (2 of 3). The r-colored vowels are decomposed into a plain
# vowel plus a real rhotic FIRST, so that the single statement below --
# English /ɹ/ is the Spanish tap -- reaches the rhotic that decomposition
# produced. Run the other way round, 'butter' comes out [bateɹ] with an
# English approximant still in it, because the tapping rule has already
# looked and the /ɹ/ arrives after it. This is the feeding relation the
# ordered cascade exists for, and it is the reason the tap is stated once
# instead of twice.
ɚ -> eɹ ; unstressed r-colored vowel decomposes
ɝ -> eɹ ; stressed r-colored vowel decomposes

# ORDERING (3 of 3), and it is the specific-before-general shape the
# shipped japanese-moraic set uses for its epenthetic vowels: the trill
# rules take their sites first, and the tap rule below is then correct
# with no exceptions written into it. Run the other way round there is no
# /ɹ/ left for them to see and every rhotic is a tap.
#
# WHERE THE TRILL GOES is not free variation and not emphasis: the two
# rhotics contrast only between vowels, and everywhere else the choice is
# positional. Word-initially it is the trill -- 'rata', 'rojo', 'Roma' --
# and so is a rhotic after a heterosyllabic /n l s/: 'honra', 'alrededor',
# 'Israel'. MEASURED, with only the tap rule: 'ɹˈæt' -> [ɾˈat], which is
# 'rata' pronounced as if it were spelled with a single r, and no Spanish
# speaker produces it.
#
# The three left contexts are LITERALS and cannot be a class: /n l s/ are
# a nasal, a lateral approximant and a fricative, sharing only a place
# that /t d/ share too -- and 'tres' is [tɾes], a tap. '[place=alveolar]'
# would take the cluster onsets with it. This is the same situation as
# 'the segments Spanish does not have' above: what the three have in
# common is a position in the syllable, which is not a feature.
ɹ -> r / # _ ; ɹ is the trill word-initially
ɹ -> r / n _ ; ɹ is the trill after n
ɹ -> r / l _ ; ɹ is the trill after l
ɹ -> r / s _ ; ɹ is the trill after s
ɹ -> ɾ ; ɹ is the tap elsewhere

# --- five vowels --------------------------------------------------------
# Spanish has /i e a o u/ and no length or tenseness distinction, so the
# English system collapses onto it. Literals again, and for a stronger
# reason than above: this is a MAPPING, one source vowel at a time, and
# the target of each line is not predictable from the source's features
# by any single feature change. A feature-change rule over '[vowel]'
# would also be free to reach the epenthetic /e/ that prothesis just
# inserted; a literal cannot, because /e/ is not on the left of any line
# here. Prothesis is ordered first partly to make that visible: if a
# later rule ever does rewrite /e/, the tests will show 'eskul' moving.
ɪ -> i ; ɪ is i
ɛ -> e ; ɛ is e
æ -> a ; æ is a
ʌ -> a ; ʌ is a
ə -> a ; ə is a
ɑ -> a ; ɑ is a
ɔ -> o ; ɔ is o
ʊ -> u ; ʊ is u

# Diphthongs are left alone. Spanish has /ei ai oi au/ as vowel
# sequences, so the English diphthongs need no repair -- and if they are
# written TIED they are single units and none of the lines above reaches
# them, while written untied their second elements are /ɪ ʊ/ and reduce
# to /i u/, which is the same answer. That the two spellings agree here
# is a fact about this set, not a general one: see the note at the end of
# american-english.rules for a case where they do not.

# --- deliberately not modeled ------------------------------------------
# Named so the gaps are known rather than discovered.
#
# * Spirantisation. Spanish /b d ɡ/ are [β ð ɣ] except after a pause or a
#   nasal, so 'bebé' is [beˈβe]. That is an allophonic rule of Spanish
#   proper, the same kind of thing american-english.rules does, and it
#   belongs to a Spanish narrow-transcription set rather than to an
#   adaptation set. Adding it here would also undo 'ð -> d' above on
#   exactly the segments that rule just produced, which would be a real
#   ordering trap rather than an interesting one.
# * Final-obstruent devoicing and cluster reduction in codas: Spanish
#   permits very little in a coda, so 'texts' is repaired far more
#   heavily than anything here does.
# * Stress. Spanish stress rules would move the accent in many of these
#   words, and stress is not writable in this notation at all
#   (docs/rules.md, "Known limits"), so no rule here could try.
# * /ʒ/ and /d͡ʒ/. Both are handled by Spanish speakers in ways that vary
#   by dialect -- [j] [ʝ] [d͡ʒ] [ʃ] are all attested -- so there is no
#   single systematic answer to state.
